---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const head = assertion('six-thousand-six-hundred-a-head')
const ss = assertion('four-social-security-programmes')
const roads = assertion('the-biggest-federal-grant-here-is-roads')
const two = assertion('one-billion-and-one-hundred-and-fifty-five-million')
const dod = assertion('ninety-seven-per-cent-defence')
const four = assertion('four-firms-and-one-is-from-here')
const saudi = assertion('forty-two-tanks-for-saudi-arabia')
const forge = assertion('the-forge-and-the-refinery')

const spec = (a: typeof ss, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const ssSpec = spec(ss, 'Dollars paid into Allen County, FY2025')
const fourSpec = spec(four, 'Contract dollars performed here, 2008–2025')
---

<Article slug="two-addresses-for-one-dollar">
  <p>
    Allen County's own government runs on $374,841,547 a year, and its books are already here. The
    larger set of books over them was not. The United States publishes every dollar it awards, and
    it can be asked by county.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={head} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ss} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="ss"
    title="What a federal budget mostly does here"
    caption="Four Social Security programmes, federal year 2025, for work and payments performed in Allen County. Together they are four fifths of the federal year and $5,318 for every resident."
    columns={['Programme', 'Dollars']}
    rows={ss.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `$${f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="ss" set:html={JSON.stringify(ssSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    So the single largest thing the federal government does in this county is send cheques to old
    people, and it does it at a scale a county budget cannot approach. Veterans' compensation for
    service-connected disability adds another $40,574,698 on top.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={roads} />

  <h2>The same question, twice</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={two} />
  <Gloss>
    A geography field in a query looks like a formality. It is the whole answer. Set the scope to
    where the work was done and this county's federal contract economy is $1.09 billion; set it to
    where the payee's address is and it is $155 million. Neither is an error, and the file prefers
    neither. "How much does the federal government spend in Allen County" is two questions — how
    much work does it buy here, and how much money do people and firms here receive — and the
    ratio between them, 7.03 on contracts, is itself the measurement: it is how much of this
    county's federal economy is worked here and banked elsewhere.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={dod} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={four} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="four"
    title="Four firms, ninety-two per cent"
    caption="Federal contract dollars performed in Allen County, 2008–2025, by performer. Only Superior Forge & Steel appears when the same file is asked who was paid."
    columns={['Performer', 'Dollars performed here']}
    rows={four.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `$${f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')}`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="four" set:html={JSON.stringify(fourSpec)} is:inline />

  <h2>What the contracts are for</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={saudi} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={forge} />
  <p>
    Which is the county described by its own invoices: a government arsenal building tanks for a
    foreign buyer, a refinery selling kerosene to the Defense Logistics Agency, a forge turning out
    the casings for the largest conventional bomb in the American arsenal, and a twenty-two-year
    contract to run the arsenal's boiler house until 2038. Three of those four are paid to addresses
    in other states. The fourth is a Lima company, and it is the only one of them this county's
    other records would have found on their own.
  </p>
</Article>
